WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.478
Picture a car whose speedometer has broken.

2
00:00:05.478 --> 00:00:11.033
Only the odometer still works, the counter of every kilometre the car has ever driven.

3
00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:14.242
It reads fifty two thousand three hundred and ten.

4
00:00:14.242 --> 00:00:15.611
Are you going too fast?

5
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:17.478
The number cannot tell you.

6
00:00:17.478 --> 00:00:21.863
It is precise, and it keeps moving, and it says nothing about right now.

7
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:23.108
But note it now.

8
00:00:23.108 --> 00:00:24.995
Note it again a minute later.

9
00:00:24.995 --> 00:00:27.207
Divide the difference by the time.

10
00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:29.380
One kilometre in one minute.

11
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.118
Sixty kilometres an hour.

12
00:00:31.118 --> 00:00:32.787
Now you know your speed.

13
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.088
Our app's counters are odometers.

14
00:00:36.088 --> 00:00:42.852
Ask Prometheus for the bare name, hello responses total, and you get three answers, one per label set.

15
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:46.350
Three hundred and three requests since the app started.

16
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.797
That says almost nothing about right now.

17
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.932
One value per series, all for the same moment.

18
00:00:51.932 --> 00:00:56.203
It is like asking every car in a car park for its odometer reading.

19
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:02.131
Add a time range in square brackets, one minute, and you get every reading in that window.

20
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:07.393
Prometheus reads the app every fifteen seconds, so a minute holds four readings.

21
00:01:07.393 --> 00:01:11.883
Ninety nine, one sixty seven, two thirty five, three hundred and three.

22
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.499
That is called a range vector: every reading in the window.

23
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.699
Now do what we did in the car.

24
00:01:17.699 --> 00:01:20.897
Last minus first: two hundred and four requests.

25
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.802
Over the forty five seconds between them.

26
00:01:23.802 --> 00:01:26.438
About four and a half requests a second.

27
00:01:26.438 --> 00:01:26.965
A speed.

28
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:28.728
You never do that by hand.

29
00:01:28.728 --> 00:01:34.135
The function rate does the subtraction and the division for you, for every series at once.

30
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:38.789
rate: the average growth per second of a counter, over a time range.

31
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.701
Now a small change.

32
00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:45.359
Same scrape every fifteen seconds, but a window of only ten seconds.

33
00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:46.865
What does this return?

34
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:53.477
Slide a ten second window along the readings.

35
00:01:53.477 --> 00:01:55.216
It usually holds only one.

36
00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:58.855
One reading, nothing to subtract.

37
00:01:58.855 --> 00:02:02.230
So the series quietly drops out of the result.

38
00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.551
No error, no zero.

39
00:02:03.551 --> 00:02:04.652
Nothing at all.

40
00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:08.735
Well, that is a query that looks right and answers with silence.

41
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:10.615
The fix is a longer window.

42
00:02:10.615 --> 00:02:13.300
Use at least four times the scrape interval.

43
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.315
With fifteen seconds, one minute.

44
00:02:15.315 --> 00:02:19.404
It needs two readings, and four leave enough when one scrape fails.

45
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:22.375
That silence has a cousin.

46
00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:26.037
Before the app had ever failed, ask for its server errors.

47
00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:27.480
You get nothing.

48
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.162
Not zero.

49
00:02:28.162 --> 00:02:31.727
No series with a five hundred label exists yet.

50
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:34.952
And any maths with an empty side is empty too.

51
00:02:34.952 --> 00:02:38.496
An error ratio shows nothing, where you expected zero.

52
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.528
Add or vector zero to the error part, and you get a real zero.

53
00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:48.271
It is the difference between a chart that says no errors, and a chart with the page torn out.

54
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.216
Odometers have one more trick.

55
00:02:51.216 --> 00:02:54.664
When the app restarts, its counter falls back to zero.

56
00:02:54.664 --> 00:02:59.453
In one test, it went from two thousand three hundred and seventy to eleven.

57
00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:07.103
rate reads any drop as a restart, like a mechanic who sees a lower reading and knows the odometer was replaced.

58
00:03:07.103 --> 00:03:10.453
Those eleven count as new requests, not as a fall.

59
00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:15.831
Now run three copies of the app, like a taxi fleet, and you want the total.

60
00:03:15.831 --> 00:03:17.165
Two ways to write it.

61
00:03:17.165 --> 00:03:19.896
Add the counters first, then take the rate.

62
00:03:19.896 --> 00:03:21.675
Or take each rate, then add.

63
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.930
Let's steelman the first.

64
00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:25.067
It looks tidy.

65
00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:26.528
One sum, one rate.

66
00:03:26.733 --> 00:03:27.920
One copy restarts.

67
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.358
Its counter drops, so the total dips.

68
00:03:30.358 --> 00:03:35.564
And rate, looking at the total, mistakes that dip for a reset of the whole sum.

69
00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:38.333
You get a spike of traffic that never happened.

70
00:03:38.567 --> 00:03:43.075
Take each copy's rate first, where a restart is visible, and then add.

71
00:03:43.075 --> 00:03:44.169
Steady, and true.

72
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.188
So the rule.

73
00:03:45.188 --> 00:03:48.666
Take the rate of each series first, then add them up.

74
00:03:48.666 --> 00:03:50.372
Never the other way round.

75
00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.242
So the moral of the story.

76
00:03:53.242 --> 00:03:56.146
A counter on its own says almost nothing.

77
00:03:56.146 --> 00:03:57.351
Ask for its rate.

78
00:03:57.567 --> 00:04:01.154
Odometer to speed: last minus first, over the time.

79
00:04:01.154 --> 00:04:04.531
A window that is too short answers with silence.

80
00:04:04.531 --> 00:04:05.797
Empty is not zero.

81
00:04:05.797 --> 00:04:07.556
And rate first, then add.

82
00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:09.256
A quick check.

83
00:04:09.256 --> 00:04:12.154
Prometheus scrapes every fifteen seconds.

84
00:04:12.154 --> 00:04:14.345
Is a thirty second window safe?

85
00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:18.143
Too short.

86
00:04:18.143 --> 00:04:22.607
It holds about two readings, so one failed scrape leaves only one.

87
00:04:22.607 --> 00:04:25.380
Four times fifteen seconds is one minute.
